-- Retroactive application of the new MAX_ACTIVE_JOBS_PER_COMPANY cap
-- (30 active jobs/company, see JOB_ENGINE_ARCHITECTURE.md's dated entry for
-- this change and supabase/functions/_shared/pipeline/companyCap.ts's own
-- header comment for the full tiering rationale). Part 2 of the 2026-08-27
-- rescope -- Part 1 (20260825190000) fixed *what kind* of job is relevant;
-- this fixes *how many* postings from one employer are allowed to crowd out
-- everyone else, per the user's explicit direction: "I want to focus more
-- on having more companies rather than few companies with tons of jobs...
-- Pick the most important/relevant ones to consulting, investment banking,
-- tech, finance, and similar fields."
--
-- Deactivates (active=false, status='removed'), never deletes -- these are
-- valid, real, relevant postings merely over quota, categorically different
-- from Part 1's targets (categorically wrong postings for this product).
-- 'removed' is the same job_status value resolve-duplicate-candidate uses
-- for "taken out of the active set for a reason other than the listing
-- itself disappearing" (confirmed valid: job_status enum includes
-- 'active'/'expiring_soon'/'potentially_expired'/'expired'/'removed').
--
-- Every id below was computed client-side by running the exact deployed
-- tiering/ranking logic (companyCap.ts's tierForJobFunction +
-- compareCapCandidates, reimplemented 1:1 against a live pull of every
-- active job's id/company/job_function name/quality_score/posted_date) --
-- not reimplemented in SQL -- same precedent as Part 1's migration
-- (20260825190000) and 20260824120000 before it, avoiding any risk of a
-- mismatch between what was reviewed and what actually got deactivated.
--
-- Real live counts before this migration (2026-08-31):
--   - Stripe: 518 -> 30 active (488 deactivated; survivors 30 tier-0 / 0 tier-1 / 0 unclassified)
--   - Carvana: 404 -> 30 active (374 deactivated; survivors 6 tier-0 / 24 tier-1 / 0 unclassified)
--   - Databricks: 322 -> 30 active (292 deactivated; survivors 14 tier-0 / 9 tier-1 / 7 unclassified)
--   - Brex: 170 -> 30 active (140 deactivated; survivors 22 tier-0 / 8 tier-1 / 0 unclassified)
--   - Charlie Health: 166 -> 30 active (136 deactivated; survivors 7 tier-0 / 0 tier-1 / 23 unclassified)
--   - IMC: 165 -> 30 active (135 deactivated; survivors 30 tier-0 / 0 tier-1 / 0 unclassified)
--   - Figma: 142 -> 30 active (112 deactivated; survivors 30 tier-0 / 0 tier-1 / 0 unclassified)
--   - Airbnb: 91 -> 30 active (61 deactivated; survivors 9 tier-0 / 13 tier-1 / 8 unclassified)
--   - Coinbase: 87 -> 30 active (57 deactivated; survivors 13 tier-0 / 8 tier-1 / 9 unclassified)
--   - Robinhood: 49 -> 30 active (19 deactivated; survivors 9 tier-0 / 7 tier-1 / 14 unclassified)
--   - Deloitte: 40 -> 30 active (10 deactivated; survivors 25 tier-0 / 0 tier-1 / 5 unclassified)
--
-- Unchanged (already at or under the cap): Guild.
--
-- Total: 2156 -> 332 active across all 12 companies (1824 deactivated).
--
-- No FK/cascade concerns: unlike Part 1's delete-based cleanup, this is a
-- plain UPDATE against jobs.active/jobs.status -- job_sources and
-- duplicate_candidates rows for these postings are untouched (the postings
-- still exist, just deactivated), so there's nothing to cascade or
-- pre-delete here.

